<h2>How the trial reached this point</h2>
<p>Notice of accusation in the cheque complaint was framed in April 2022. The complainant entered the witness box and was examined-in-chief, with cross-examination begun in mid-2023 but left incomplete. What followed, on the record summarised in the judgment, was a long sequence of adjournments and extensions, several granted expressly as last opportunities. In January 2026, with no appearance to conduct the remaining cross-examination, the trial court closed that chapter and listed the matter for the next stage. The application to recall the complainant under Section 348 BNSS came after that closure and was dismissed by a reasoned order, which the accused then challenged before the High Court.</p>

<h2>The two procedural gates</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Gate one: Section 438(2) BNSS.</strong> Revision does not lie against interlocutory orders. An order refusing recall of a witness is interlocutory, so the revisional route was closed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Gate two: inherent jurisdiction.</strong> The High Court may still interfere where an order works gross injustice. The court examined the record for any such injustice and found none, relying on its own recent decisions applying the same standard.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment (para 9):</strong> &ldquo;passovers and adjournments are matter of courtesy extended by the Court to the counsel&rdquo; — a courtesy that cannot ripen into a right to keep a witness perpetually available.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the recall plea failed</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s reasons were cumulative. The complainant had already faced substantial cross-examination. The trial court had accommodated the defence over an extended period, well beyond the norm for a summary-character trial. The default on the final date was not satisfactorily explained. And nothing identified any specific, essential question that remained unput — the application read as an effort to prolong the proceedings rather than to meet a genuine evidentiary need. On that assessment, the High Court dismissed the petition as devoid of merit and directed deposit of Rs 10,000 as costs within a week, with a copy of the order to be sent to the trial court.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence.</strong> Treat every listed cross-examination date as the only one. If recall becomes necessary, the application must spell out the specific questions or documents that make further examination essential — generalities invite dismissal and costs.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For complainants.</strong> A record of attendance and readiness on each date is the best answer to a late recall plea. Where the defence has defaulted after indulgence, seek closure and oppose reopening by pointing to the chronology itself.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For both sides.</strong> The interlocutory bar means most mid-trial grievances in cheque cases must wait for the final outcome; running to the High Court over each procedural order risks costs and no relief.</div>
</div>
